{"step_index":1619,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-19T19:31:11Z","input_tokens":2726,"cache_read_tokens":189104,"output_tokens":847,"content":"**The honest answer: Mathematically yes, but in reality no — here is why:**\n\n### 1. The Math\nThe math uses real Bybit prices, strict **0.75% position sizing** (\\$375 risk), and **1:3 Risk/Reward** (+\\$1,125 win / -\\$375 loss). If every trade executed with 100% perfection, that is what the gross balance would be.\n\n### 2. The 3 \"Real Money\" Gaps\nIn live trading with real cash, 3 frictions reduce the actual profit:\n- **Exchange Fees & Funding**: Bybit charges ~0.055% taker fees + 8h funding rates. Over 50 trades, fees eat **10%–20% of net profits**.\n- **Slippage & Queue**: On paper, orders fill instantly. In real order books, high-speed volatility causes 0.05%–0.2% price slippage.\n- **Partial Fills**: In fast breakouts, you don't always get filled for the full contract size.\n\n> **Rule of Thumb:** A profitable paper system generally yields **60%–75% of its paper profit** when transitioned to real capital due to fees and slippage. That is why running this 30-day forward test first is critical before risking a single dollar.","thinking":"The core question is about the equivalence of demo account profits to real-world profits. The user wants to know if the demo's profit figures would accurately reflect real financial gains if the demo trades were executed with actual capital.\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\n"}
